t actually, since the chow is 1-2-3, the middle tile is 2. So if I discard B2, I still have B1, and if I draw B3, I can form B1-2-3. Alternatively, if I discard B1, I have B2 and need to draw B3. Either way, the chance is similar. Maybe playing B2 is better because it's the middle, but I'm not sure. Alternatively, playing B1 might be better because if I have B2 in hand, it's more likely to form the chow if I draw B3.\n\nWait, if I play B1, I have B2 in hand. If I draw B3, I can form B1-2-3. If I play B2, I have B1 in hand. If I draw B3, I can form B1-2-3. So both are the same. So the choice between B1 and B2 is arbitrary. However, looking at the valid actions, the options include Play B1 and Play B2. But in the given valid actions, the user lists Play B1 as an option. Let me check the valid actions again.\n\nThe valid actions are: [Play T3; Play W3; Play J2; Play T9; Play F3; Play T4; Play B9; Play B7; Play B2; Play T8; Play B1; Play W9; Play W4].\n\nSo both Play B1 and Play B2 are available. Which one to choose? Since they are both equally good, maybe choose the one that's part of a more flexible structure. Alternatively, maybe consider the unshown tiles. The B1 has 4 left, B2 has 4 left. No difference. So it's a toss-up. However, in the current hand, the B1 is a terminal tile, and B2 is a simple tile. Maybe keeping the terminal tile (B1) in hand is better for other potential melds, but in this case, it's part of the chow. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the best move is to play B2. Because if I play B2, I still have B1, which is a terminal tile. Terminal tiles are more valuable in certain patterns like All Terminals. But since I don't have many terminals, maybe not. Alternatively, playing B1 to keep B2 as part of the potential chow.\n\nHmm. This is tricky. Another angle: the current hand has two B9s. If I play B1, the B9 pair is still there. If I play B2, same. The pair is important for the hand structure. So maybe it's better to keep the pair intact by not disc